[38;5;252m[1m## One harness bug worth noting[0m[0m
[0m[0m
My first implementation captured output by [38;5;10mdup2[0m-ing fd 1 in-process. That flaked: libtest writes its own progress lines ("test capture_is_not_vacuous ... ") to fd 1 from a different thread, and those landed in the capture file. A mutex can't fix this because libtest's printing isn't under the harness's control. I replaced it with fork-isolated capture — the child redirects its own fd 1, flushes the inherited stdout buffer to [38;5;10m/dev/null[0m first, then calls [38;5;10mdriver[0m — and the parent's fd 1 is never touched. 25 consecutive parallel runs pass with zero failures.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
To confirm the tests aren't vacuously passing, I injected defects into the Rust source (dropping [38;5;10m0x0b[0m from the [38;5;10mspace[0m class bits, and skipping [38;5;10m'z'[0m in [38;5;10mtoupper[0m); both were caught with the exact offending input and line identified. [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m was restored and byte-compared against its backup afterward.[0m[0m
